+## Key Vault Workflow
+
+`mac-keyvault` keeps P-256 key pairs in the current user's login keychain as permanent, non-extractable items (`kSecAttrIsPermanent`, `kSecAttrIsExtractable=false`) whose private-key ACL is a legacy `SecAccess` trusted-application list containing only the calling binary. Every label lives under `works.relux.mac-keyvault.`; a short name is placed under that prefix automatically and the tool never creates, lists, or deletes anything outside it.
+
+```bash
+mac-keyvault init signer                      # works.relux.mac-keyvault.signer in the login keychain
+mac-keyvault --json list                      # label, store, created, base64url(SHA-256(SPKI DER))
+mac-keyvault pub signer --out .temp/signer.pem  # .pem -> PEM, anything else -> SPKI DER
+mac-keyvault rotate signer                    # creates works.relux.mac-keyvault.signer.v2, keeps the old key
+mac-keyvault delete signer --confirm          # refused without --confirm
+```
+
+Exit codes: `0` ok, `1` operational failure (Security.framework OSStatus, unknown label, I/O), `2` usage, `3` refused by a policy gate (`duplicate_label`, `confirmation_required`, `foreign_label`, `invalid_label`, `enclave_unavailable`, `user_presence_unavailable`). `--json` wraps every answer in `{ok, command, result|error}`; refusals carry `os_status` when Security.framework produced them.
+
+Store decision (recorded on `EPIC-260915-1g0e1c_secure-enclave-probe.md` and re-verified while building the tool): an ad-hoc signed CLI gets `-34018 errSecMissingEntitlement` for a permanent Secure Enclave key, for any `kSecAttrAccessControl`-based ACL (it routes the item into the Data Protection keychain), and for `kSecAccessControlUserPresence`. `--enclave` and `--user-presence` therefore try the real Security call and refuse with that status and reason; there is no silent keychain fallback. Persisting Secure Enclave keys needs a signed helper with a provisioning profile and is a separate follow-up. The legacy key schema has no creation-date attribute, so the creation time and store are stored in the item's application tag; a key whose tag or public half cannot be read is listed with `unknown` values instead of a guessed one.
+
+The private-key ACL is bound to the exact binary that created the item; a rebuilt `mac-keyvault` still lists, exports, rotates, and deletes keys without a prompt, but private-key use from a different binary is subject to the keychain's own authorization prompt. Tests use only `works.relux.mac-keyvault.test.` labels and delete them in cleanup; a guard wrapper fails the suite before any other label reaches the keychain.
+
